{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And you shall see.</b> I have already informed you that the men of the holy tongue do not preserve the words but only the meanings; therefore we need not investigate plene and defective spelling, as I will explain further. Now Jethro mentioned men of valor — who have strength to bear burden and do not fear them. And corresponding to 'men of valor,' Moses said when he recounted the matter: 'Take for yourselves men' (Deuteronomy 1:13), like 'choose for us men' (Exodus 17:9), 'all of them men' (Numbers 13:3)." "And Jethro said 'fearers of Elohim' — who have no fear of man but of HaShem alone. And corresponding to them Moses said 'wise and discerning' (Deuteronomy 1:13), for it is impossible to be a fearer of Heaven as fitting except one who is wise." "And Jethro said 'men of truth' — who are not liars. <b>Haters of unjust gain</b> — money. And they are known as such by the sight of a man's eye. And corresponding to them Moses said 'and known' (Deuteronomy 1:13)." "<b>Chiefs of thousands.</b> One should wonder: if these chiefs are interpreted according to their plain meaning, their number would be more than seventy-nine thousand, and this is extremely far-fetched — to have that many chiefs. And Scripture says: 'For the transgression of a land, many are its chiefs' (Proverbs 28:2). Furthermore, how is it possible that an eighth of the camp would be heads of tribes? For thus Moses said: 'And I took the heads of your tribes' (Deuteronomy 1:15). And you would find in them all the good qualities mentioned. And they are of those who came out of Egypt, who learned their deeds, and it is written: 'After the deeds of the land of Egypt' (Leviticus 18:3). Now the wilderness generation, whom Moses taught for forty years, and who had no need to practice a trade, for their bread was given, and their waters were reliable, and the manna was clear, unlike the heretics who craved what they were not accustomed to eat in Egypt. And Moses said to them in the fortieth year: 'And HaShem has not given you a heart to know' (Deuteronomy 29:3). So one should wonder: how could the aforementioned number be found, such that all of them would be wise and discerning? And the correct view in my eyes is that 'chiefs of thousands' are those under whose hand are a thousand men — his servants or his young men or his hired laborers. Perhaps these are the heads of the tribes, and their number was twelve. And the chiefs of hundreds are many, and chiefs of fifties — in the manner of 'and fifty men running before him' (II Samuel 15:1)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {3 "The word המינים appears to mean 'heretics' in Ibn Ezra's usage here; the clause contrasts the manna (described as מפקח, 'clear') with those who craved non-habitual food, reading המינים literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:49.832377662Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}